{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"6483271d2666c436","name":"card_event_frontal_defense","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Poland/events/card_event_frontal_defense","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FRONTAL DEFENSE","text":"Give a friendly unit: \"At the end of your turn, deal 1 damage to all units.\"","flavorText":"Poland’s Plan West was shaped by the need to defend its western industrial regions and the belief that retreating might lead Britain and France to negotiate peace with Germany, as in the Munich Agreement in 1938. Despite French advice to fall back behind natural barriers like the Vistula and San Rivers, Poland positioned its forces along the German-Polish border, which left them vulnerable to fast-moving German attacks. The plan aimed for a gradual retreat, in order to buy time for full mobilization and an eventual counteroffensive with Allied support. However, poor mobility and weak supply lines prevented such maneuvering and Polish forces struggled against superior German mechanized units. On top of that, civilian panic and misinformation disrupted the Polish defense efforts. While Poland expected a quick Allied intervention, the Western powers anticipated a prolonged war, leading to Poland’s rapid defeat in September 1939.","faction":"Poland","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_frontal_defense","title":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_frontal_defense_title","SourceString":"FRONTAL DEFENSE","LocalizedString":"FRONTAL DEFENSE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Poland","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_frontal_defense_text","SourceString":"Give a friendly unit: \"At the end of your turn, deal 1 damage to all units.\"","LocalizedString":"Give a friendly unit: \"At the end of your turn, deal 1 damage to all units.\""},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_frontal_defense_history","SourceString":"Poland’s Plan West was shaped by the need to defend its western industrial regions and the belief that retreating might lead Britain and France to negotiate peace with Germany, as in the Munich Agreement in 1938. Despite French advice to fall back behind natural barriers like the Vistula and San Rivers, Poland positioned its forces along the German-Polish border, which left them vulnerable to fast-moving German attacks. The plan aimed for a gradual retreat, in order to buy time for full mobilization and an eventual counteroffensive with Allied support. However, poor mobility and weak supply lines prevented such maneuvering and Polish forces struggled against superior German mechanized units. On top of that, civilian panic and misinformation disrupted the Polish defense efforts. While Poland expected a quick Allied intervention, the Western powers anticipated a prolonged war, leading to Poland’s rapid defeat in September 1939.","LocalizedString":"Poland’s Plan West was shaped by the need to defend its western industrial regions and the belief that retreating might lead Britain and France to negotiate peace with Germany, as in the Munich Agreement in 1938. Despite French advice to fall back behind natural barriers like the Vistula and San Rivers, Poland positioned its forces along the German-Polish border, which left them vulnerable to fast-moving German attacks. The plan aimed for a gradual retreat, in order to buy time for full mobilization and an eventual counteroffensive with Allied support. However, poor mobility and weak supply lines prevented such maneuvering and Polish forces struggled against superior German mechanized units. On top of that, civilian panic and misinformation disrupted the Polish defense efforts. While Poland expected a quick Allied intervention, the Western powers anticipated a prolonged war, leading to Poland’s rapid defeat in September 1939."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPl